---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="The engineering path and current platform focus of Joshua Brewer."
  ><section class="page-hero shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p><h1>Systems thinker.<br />Platform builder.</h1><p class="lede">
      Seven-plus years across systems, cloud, security, and platform environments have shaped a
      practical approach: understand the system, reduce the friction, automate the repeatable parts,
      and document the path for the next operator.
    </p>
  </section><div class="shell stack">
    <section>
      <SectionHeading title="A progression, not a pivot" /><div class="story-grid">
        <p>
          My work began in systems administration, enterprise infrastructure, security operations,
          and virtualization. That foundation led to AWS container support, Kubernetes troubleshooting,
          and then platform engineering: building the paved roads that help other engineers deliver
          safely and quickly.
        </p><p>
          The daily work now spans AWS, EKS, Terraform, Atmos, UDS/Zarf, GitLab CI/CD, CodePipeline,
          Grafana, Keycloak, PostgreSQL, and operational documentation. I am also pursuing an M.S. in
          Management Information Systems at Texas A&amp;M to connect platform operations with analytics,
          data systems, and organizational decision-making.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section><section>
      <SectionHeading eyebrow="Recent focus" title="The problems I keep choosing" /><div
        class="pillar-grid"
      >
        <article class="pillar">
          <h3>Multi-tenant observability</h3><p>
            Grafana org isolation, Keycloak-backed access, Terraform-managed dashboards, and clear
            onboarding flows for teams and applications.
          </p>
        </article><article class="pillar">
          <h3>Repeatable platform delivery</h3><p>
            Atmos/Terraform patterns, workflow ordering, schema validation, policy checks, release
            lines, and environment promotion.
          </p>
        </article><article class="pillar">
          <h3>Operational knowledge capture</h3><p>
            Runbooks, onboarding docs, outage response guidance, restore testing, issue templates,
            and decision records that turn hard-won context into team capability.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section><section>
      <SectionHeading eyebrow="Education" title="Learning in public, with purpose" /><div
        class="credentials"
      >
        <article>
          <h3>M.S. Management Information Systems</h3><p>
            Texas A&amp;M University · Mays Business School
          </p><span>2026 – Present</span>
        </article><article>
          <h3>M.S. Cloud Computing Architecture</h3><p>University of Maryland Global Campus</p><span
            >2021</span
          >
        </article><article>
          <h3>Graduate Certificate, Penetration Testing and Ethical Hacking</h3><p>
            SANS Technology Institute
          </p><span>2024</span>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section><section>
      <SectionHeading title="Engineering values" /><div class="pillar-grid">
        <article class="pillar">
          <h3>Make complexity legible</h3><p>
            Good platforms make hidden dependencies visible and give engineers a trustworthy path to
            operate the system.
          </p>
        </article><article class="pillar">
          <h3>Prefer evidence</h3><p>
            Metrics, logs, traces, tests, and clear documentation beat hunches during incidents and
            architectural decisions.
          </p>
        </article><article class="pillar">
          <h3>Design for the next operator</h3><p>
            Automation, defaults, and runbooks turn a one-time fix into a durable operating model.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div></BaseLayout
>
